# Copy to .env. Never commit .env.
# Phase 1 development database (feature-specs/04 § 2). Docker: docker compose up db.
# Without Docker: a local PostgreSQL 16+ with a superuser hb/hb and a database harness_bible
# (for example binaries in %USERPROFILE%\pg17 and a cluster in %USERPROFILE%\pgdata\hb17, started with
# `pg_ctl start -D %USERPROFILE%\pgdata\hb17 -l %USERPROFILE%\pgdata\hb17.log`).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://hb:hb@localhost:5432/harness_bible

# Path to the book checkout (bible/ with book/ and labs/), relative to this folder.
BOOK_DIR=../bible

# Release id the pipeline ingests as.
RELEASE_ID=2026.09

# Optional: CI's render hash; ingestion aborts when the recomputed hash differs.
# RENDER_HASH_EXPECTED=

# This deployment's origin: OIDC redirect URI, allowed origins, CSP form-action (feature-specs/08 § 5.6).
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Keycloak realm issuer and the hb-web client (feature-specs/09 § 3.2, § 10; infra/keycloak/README.md).
# `npm run auth:kc` starts the local Keycloak with this secret; `npm run auth:kc:fake` stands in for
# Google and GitHub; `npm run auth:kc:walk` drives the whole handshake. The Google and GitHub secrets
# live in Keycloak's vault, never here. All three unset = the sign-in page offers only the dev provider.
KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=http://localhost:8081/realms/harness-bible
KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=hb-web
KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=dev-hb-web-secret

# Optional pilot allow-list: e-mails and @domain entries, comma-separated; empty allows everyone.
# AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS=@example.org,someone@example.com

# Development only (refused in production): GET /api/v1/auth/dev?email=dev@harness-bible.local
# signs in without Keycloak (feature-specs/08 § 5.7). `npm run db:seed` creates that user.
AUTH_DEV_PROVIDER=1

# OPA sidecar (feature-specs/09 § 5.1); unset = every policy decision denies, so every write
# refuses. `npm run dev` starts it beside Next (the `opa` binary on PATH); `npm run policy:serve` alone.
OPA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8181

# Salt for the hashed IPs in AuthEvent (feature-specs/09 § 8.6); rotate in production.
# IP_HASH_SALT=

# The decision-aid runner (lib/aids/run.ts): `docker` runs the chapter's script in the lab tier's
# container (no network, read-only root, 2 cpu, 4 GiB, the host user rather than root where the host has users);
# `python` runs a local subprocess without Docker.
# AID_RUNNER=docker
# DOCKER_BIN=docker
# AID_IMAGE=python:3.12-slim
# Windows python: %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Python\Python312\python.exe (winget Python.Python.3.12, user scope).
# PYTHON_BIN=python

# The Ed25519 private key that signs ledger export trailers (feature-specs/14 § 7), as one PEM in double
# quotes with \n between its lines. `npm run ledger:keygen` writes the pair under infra/ledger/ and never
# prints the private key: copy infra/ledger/export-private.pem into this line yourself. Without it
# `npm run ledger:export` refuses (LEDGER_KEY_MISSING) and appends still work.
# LEDGER_EXPORT_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# The model gateway (feature-specs/11 § 3, § 8): `docker compose up db gateway` runs LiteLLM on 4000 from
# infra/gateway/litellm.yaml. Its provider and Langfuse keys live in infra/gateway/.env (git-ignored; see
# infra/gateway/.env.example) and never here. LITELLM_MASTER_KEY mints virtual keys: compose hands this value to
# the gateway container and the workers read it (units 25c and 25d). Generate it locally and never commit it:
# node -e "console.log('sk-'+require('crypto').randomBytes(16).toString('hex'))"
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=
# The gateway's base URL for the application (lib/env.ts): LiteLLM in compose, or the stub gateway on 4001
# (`npm run gateway:stub`, started by `npm run dev` beside OPA; it mints keys under LITELLM_MASTER_KEY too,
# answers deterministically and needs no provider key; feature-specs/11 § 4). Unset = every model call refuses.
GATEWAY_URL=http://localhost:4001
# The bearer the two self-hosted model servers require (feature-specs/12 § 3): compose passes it to the
# `embeddings` and `reranker` containers, which refuse a request without it, and to the gateway, which sends it.
# Not a provider key and not a secret of anyone else's; generate it locally and never commit it:
#   node -e "console.log('inf-'+require('crypto').randomBytes(16).toString('hex'))"
INFINITY_API_KEY=
# Encrypts the virtual keys at rest (feature-specs/11 § 3.4; lib/gateway/keys.ts): 32 bytes as 64 hex characters,
# held by the workers and by the key script; the web container leaves it unset by design. Generate it locally and
# never commit it:
# node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
GATEWAY_KEY_SECRET=
# The fixed small cap of an individual account (feature-specs/11 § 3.4, D5; tokens a month) and its tokens per
# minute, read by the workers and `npm run gateway:key -- --user <id>`. Both numbers are owed by Andre (tracker
# Open Questions); while either is unset no individual key is minted (GATEWAY_INDIVIDUAL_CAP_UNSET). The web tier
# reads the cap too, only to print it on the account page's budget meter (feature-specs/21 § 4.1); unset there, the
# meter says `no cap set`.
# INDIVIDUAL_TOKEN_CAP=
# INDIVIDUAL_TOKENS_PER_MINUTE=
# The principal `npm run gateway:check` uses when it is called without --org or --user (feature-specs/11 § 3.6,
# which runs the smoke test bare): `org:<organisationId>` or `user:<userId>`, an id from your own database. Unset =
# the command asks for a flag. --org and --user still override it.
# GATEWAY_CHECK_PRINCIPAL=
# Who pays for a signed-out reader's query embedding, so that the vector half of /[lang]/search runs for everyone
# and not for signed-in readers alone (feature-specs/12 § 6, D4; Andre's answer of 2026-09-24): the same form as
# GATEWAY_CHECK_PRINCIPAL above, `org:<organisationId>` or `user:<userId>`, an id from your own database whose
# principal holds a gateway key. An anonymous visitor then spends that principal's budget and the public read rate
# limit is the only guard, so name a principal whose cap you are willing to spend that way. Unset = an anonymous
# search stays lexical and the Query instrument's vector row reads `— · unavailable`.
# GATEWAY_ANONYMOUS_PRINCIPAL=

# The principal research is charged to (feature-specs/40 § 7.1, A6): an organisation of the operator's, `org:<id>`,
# whose gateway key's max_budget is the month's research budget (A14: US$ 20). Read by the workers alone. Unset,
# research is not configured and every answered turn is skipped as UNAVAILABLE: no run, no model call.
# RESEARCH_PRINCIPAL=
# The workers (feature-specs/11 § 7, § 8; unit 25d): `npm run workers` listens at the host and port of WORKERS_URL and
# the app dials it through lib/workers/client.ts; every request carries the shared bearer WORKERS_TOKEN (D7). The
# workers hold GATEWAY_KEY_SECRET and LITELLM_MASTER_KEY above. Generate the token locally and never commit it:
# node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# A URL without a port means its protocol's own (`http://workers` is port 80, compose's internal form); the workers
# refuse to start on anything but an http: origin, without a path, a query, a fragment or a trailing slash.
WORKERS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4002
WORKERS_TOKEN=
# The workflow engine (feature-specs/28 § 3; unit 45a): Temporal. `docker compose up -d temporal-namespace` starts it
# with its schema and its namespace, and publishes its frontend on the loopback alone. A host and a port, no scheme.
# Unset = nothing that needs a workflow runs (Improve, the researcher); the reader and the assistant are unaffected.
# The compose file registers the namespace named here, and `harness-bible` when this is unset.
TEMPORAL_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:7233
TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE=harness-bible
# Before unit 26 the development database is a local cluster on 5432, so the compose db cannot bind that port
# and the gateway needs another PostgreSQL for its own `litellm` database (created with infra/gateway/init.sql):
# LITELLM_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://hb:hb@host.docker.internal:5433/litellm

# Object storage (feature-specs/32 § 5; unit 49b). A directory for now, on Andre's word of 2026-10-06 ("stored
# locally, we don't have a cloud infra"): uploads and fetched evidence live under OBJECT_STORE_DIR by key, and are
# reached only through URLs the app signs under OBJECT_STORE_SECRET (PUT and GET /api/v1/storage/{token}, ten
# minutes each, one key and one method per URL). The default directory is .store beside this file, ignored by git.
# The secret is 32 bytes as 64 hex characters; generate it locally and never commit it:
# node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Unset, the upload and storage routes answer 503 STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE; required in production.
# OBJECT_STORE_DIR=.store
OBJECT_STORE_SECRET=

# The book's Git repository (feature-specs/37 § 3; unit 54a): the one place the product writes the book, through
# pipeline/git.ts alone, on improve/* branches and by a fast-forward of main. BOOK_DIR is never written. Unset, every
# merge refuses with BOOK_REPO_UNAVAILABLE and accepted suggestions wait in MERGING. Making it is the operator's act,
# once: a copy of the book folder, initialised on main with one commit, tagged release/2026.09, and left on a detached
# HEAD so that the product may move main without touching the checked-out tree:
#   git -C <copy> init -b main && git -C <copy> add -A && git -C <copy> commit -m "The book at release 2026.09"
#   git -C <copy> tag -a release/2026.09 -m "release 2026.09" && git -C <copy> checkout --detach
# BOOK_REPO_DIR=

# The curator queue's two calibration mechanisms (feature-specs/36 § 7; unit 53e), built and off until Andre sets a
# number. CURATOR_SEED_SHARE is the share of a curator's queue that seeds may make up, under 1 (0.05 is one seed in
# twenty items); unset or 0, none is ever offered (A16). CURATOR_SAMPLE_PER_MONTH is how many merged suggestions whose
# strongest item was tier B are drawn per part each month into the "Sampled" group; unset, none (D2).
# CURATOR_SEED_SHARE=0
# CURATOR_SAMPLE_PER_MONTH=

# Telemetry (feature-specs/21 D3). Unset, as in development and CI: nothing is exported, no OpenTelemetry code loads,
# and the JSON lines on stdout are all there is. Set to a collector's OTLP/HTTP endpoint, by convention
# http://localhost:4318: the web tier, the workers and the scheduled jobs then export each line
# as a span too. No span carries a prompt, an answer, a question, an e-mail or a key.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=

# The observability stack (feature-specs/21 § 5.2), `docker compose --profile observe up -d`. All three have
# development defaults in docker-compose.yml and need a value only where the stack is more than one person's:
# the password of the dashboard's read-only database role, Grafana's admin password, and the directory whose
# *.log files the collector reads for the product's lines (the scheduled jobs' logs; infra/schedules).
# HB_METRICS_PASSWORD=
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# HB_LOG_DIR=
